  emailId: mail_005_the_beginning
  unlockDay: 5
  sender: UNKNOWN
  subject: THE BEGINNING
  body: "<b>I.</b>\nAt the first birth of the cosmos, it was vast beyond measure:
    infinite, without end, without bound, and without life. It was a plain left bare,
    a field with no forest, a great desert with no creature in it. Yet this emptiness
    was not the emptiness of nothing. It was the Void, full of potential without
    limit, a state that the tongue of common men cannot define so that they might
    hear and understand.\n\nWithin that vastness moved a force without form, as the
    breath of the cosmos. When the Universe breathed out, a cosmos was born and spread
    wide. When the Universe breathed in, all things shrank and fell away into emptiness
    again. So it turned, a cycle without end, until the hour came for the beginning
    of all things.\n\n<b>II. The First Utterance</b>\nAt the center of the vastness,
    the cosmos spoke the First Utterance. In that moment the will to create began
    to wake, like a single small point of light upon a dark canvas. The cosmos then
    had no shape, no time, and no dimension. It was holiness at its highest, where
    the First Spirit and the Primal Matter were still fused, whole and seamless,
    a oneness not yet divided. All that would ever be lay pressed small and hidden
    at this center, waiting to burst forth at the speaking of the cosmos.\n\n<b>III.
    The Second Utterance</b>\nAnd when the cosmos began to speak again, the Second
    Utterance unfolded. It parted the spirit from Primal Matter, and life-force came
    to be. As the point of light began to open, the oneness was torn into two poles.
    Thus the law of opposites arose for the first time in the cosmos.\n\nOne pole
    was the First Spirit, as a father: positive energy, and will that has no form.
    The other pole was Primal Matter, as a mother: negative energy, the ground of
    all that has shape, the vessel that would bear all things, as a woman bears a
    child.\n\nWhen the two poles were drawn apart, the strain between them struck
    a sacred spark, called the Great Prana of Evolution. It was like lightning, or
    a thread of life, stretched between the First Spirit and Primal Matter so that
    neither might break away from the other. Through it, dead matter began to tremble,
    and to wake.\n\n<b>IV. The Third Utterance</b>\nLast came the Third Utterance.
    The Great Mind of the cosmos, the all-wise, took the three and wove them together.
    Here is the true beginning of creation. The Third Utterance is the intelligence
    of the whole cosmos, the engineer and master builder who sets his hand to the
    making of the world, as a painter draws his brush across a blank white page.
    The Great Mind spread the three out, blended them, and wove them by the law of
    karma and the mechanics of the cosmos.\n\nFrom coarse Primal Matter were spread
    out the many realms, above and below, such as the world of Fourteen Realms. From
    fragments of the First Spirit, shared out and sent down, were born the souls
    of beings, of all creatures, and of the gods. From life-force came the laws of
    nature and of evolution, which drive every being to wander, round and round,
    to cross over ignorance, to learn, and to raise its spirit at last back to the
    perfect emptiness.\n\n<b>V. The Lamp-Teachers</b>\nMany Lamp-Teachers have written
    of the beginning of the cosmos as the work of a creator God, born of a thought,
    or of a single stroke of the Great Divine Being\u2019s fingertip, each in the
    divine legends of their own people. In the creations told by all these legends,
    the god speaks, he thinks, and what he thinks appears as he has said. It is like
    a divine word uttered from the mouth of God, like the truth made plain.\n\nWhat
    all the Lamp-Teachers hold in common, in writing of the waking of the cosmos,
    is this: that the Speaker who made all things borrowed the True Manifest to speak
    creation into being. Like a servant before a high lord, who must humbly ask leave
    before doing anything, the Speaker waited for permission from the truth made
    plain. Then he needed only to speak the True Manifest, and it stood ready to
    appear.\n\n<b>VI. The Three Roots</b>\nAll creation within the span of the cosmos
    rests upon the might of three primal things: the First Spirit, Primal Matter,
    and the Great Prana of Evolution.\n\nThe First Spirit is the power of awareness.
    It is pure and without form, and it stands firm as the knower and the watcher,
    unchanging.\n\nPrimal Matter is the foundation of form. It is the shell, the
    sacred vessel that holds the soul, opening a way for the spirit to show its states
    and to weave experience within the span of dimensions.\n\nThe Great Prana of
    Evolution is the bridge of life-force. It is the sacred current that runs between
    spirit and matter. It wakes the sleeping, lifeless Primal Matter, and it is the
    cog that turns the wheel of the cosmos, driving it on into evolution that has
    no end.\n\nAll the Speakers together made the Fourteen Worlds, so that every
    being, high or low, might live by the law of the truth made plain, and so evolve
    to dwell in a higher realm.\n\n<b>THE FOURTEEN WORLDS</b>\nLet me lead you to
    hear of the Fourteen Worlds, to enrich the knowledge and thought of common people.
    They must carry this knowledge to guide themselves and those who come after,
    to strive, to seek, and to evolve into beings higher than themselves, by the
    truth made plain of the Seven Worlds.\n\nThere was once a Lamp-Teacher of great
    wisdom and understanding who spoke these words:\n\n\u201CThough a form of being
    hide itself beyond what the five senses can reach, it is not the emptiness of
    nothing in the universe where we dwell. In truth, these things stand close beside
    us, woven into our life at every moment, and have never faded. They keep their
    being in the realms above, beyond the reach of wisdom and of the five senses.\u201D\n\nAs
    the Fully Awakened Buddha taught through the divine eye, the senses of man, the
    six sense-bases, are very limited in perceiving the truth of the cosmos. They
    cannot perceive the realms laid one over another. The Buddha spoke of the Opapatika,
    who truly exist and live together with common men who walk the earth, overlaid
    upon the dimension of man, but the eye of flesh is too coarse to see them. He
    taught that whoever would come to know these states must train the mind until
    the divine eye opens, or until high wisdom arises.\n\nTurn to Krishna in the
    Bhagavad Gita, in the Mahabharata, where Krishna shows his Universal Form plainly
    for Arjuna to behold, and so shows the limit of the five senses of common men.
    Krishna says to Arjuna: \u201CYou cannot see me with your eyes of flesh. Therefore
    I will give you the divine eye, that you may see my great power.\u201D (Bhagavad
    Gita, chapter 11)\n\nHindu philosophy teaches this nearness too. It holds that
    the Paramatman dwells in every atom of all things and lives together with all
    common people who walk the earth, at all times. Yet man, veiled by maya, cannot
    see it.\n\nAnd if we follow the teaching of the Lamp-Teachers of the Christian
    faith, and of the apostle Paul, the way of Christ\u2019s creation sets down a
    plain difference between the forms that the eye of flesh can see and the hidden
    dimension beyond the reach of sight, the kingdom that cannot be touched. Jesus
    said: \u201CThe kingdom of God does not come by observation, for in truth the
    kingdom of God is in the midst of you.\u201D (Luke 17:20-21) And of the realms
    above, Saint Paul explained plainly in the Bible: \u201CFor the things which
    are seen do not endure, but the things which are not seen are lasting and eternal.\u201D
    (2 Corinthians 4:18)\n\nThis echoes the philosophy of the Lamp-Teachers of the
    Seven Worlds: that what cannot be seen by the five senses is not emptiness, but
    an eternity that truly dwells.\n\nThese three Lamp-Teachers, each in the teaching
    of his own law, hold common ground with the principle of the Seven Worlds, which
    bids all common people to strive, to evolve, and to seek the True Manifest, and
    to know that there are realms beyond what the eyes of fleshly men can see, or
    what can be awakened to through the five senses. This alone is proof enough of
    the existence of all Fourteen Realms."
